Chapter 20 - Stain
Curtis stepped out of the warehouse alley, coat still dripping, red slits scanning the next block. Jeeves had gone quiet—police were already en route to the coordinates he’d dropped.
A figure waited at the mouth of the street. Red scarf. Bandaged arms. Blades at his hips. The man didn’t hide. He stood in the open, head tilted, eyes sharp under the wraps.
“You’ve been busy.” Stain’s voice carried, low and intent. “Bodies in the warehouse. Crippled thieves two streets back. No cameras. No agency. No name.” He took one step closer, the scarf shifting. “Tell me. Are you purging filth… or are you just another pretender staining the night for your own satisfaction?”
Curtis stopped. The helmet’s slits stayed level. He didn’t answer right away.
Stain’s mouth curved, not quite a smile. “All Might is the only true hero. Everyone else who wears the title for money, fame, or comfort is a fake. I cut them down so the word means something again.” His eyes narrowed on the blood still visible on Curtis’s glove. “You kill without announcing yourself. No audience. No reward. That interests me. So I’ll ask once. What conviction drives your blade?”
The street stayed empty. Sirens were still distant. Curtis’s coat shifted in the night air as he faced the Hero Killer, the low, steady pull in his chest as quiet as ever.
Curtis’s voice came even through the helmet filter.
“Fame and fortune are useless if villains can still hurt and kill the innocent. The law is broken. Too many heroes lose sight of what matters—making sure people can live without fear of being killed or abused. Heroes who only chase glory and don’t finish the job disgust me.” He paused. The red slits stayed locked on Stain. “Even All Might disgusts me.”
The street went still.
Stain’s eyes widened a fraction. The scarf shifted as his shoulders tightened. For a second the usual fire in his posture banked into something colder.
“You dare.” The words came low, almost soft. Then the intensity snapped back, sharper. “All Might is the only one who has ever embodied what a hero must be. Selfless. Unbreakable. The standard the rest of this rotting society fails to meet. And you stand there, blood on your hands, and call him disgusting?”
He drew a blade. Not fully. Just enough that the edge caught the streetlight.
“Explain yourself. Or I will decide your conviction is as false as the rest.”
Curtis didn’t move. The coat hung heavy. Jeeves stayed silent in his ear. The distant sirens grew a little louder while Stain waited, blade half-drawn, for an answer he clearly did not expect to hear.
Curtis didn’t raise his voice.
“How many of All Might’s enemies are still alive? It only takes one strong or smart quirk user to free them. Tell me—why does the Symbol of Peace let those cockroaches live?” The red slits stayed level. “That’s not justice.”
Stain’s blade cleared the sheath the rest of the way. The point angled toward the pavement, not yet raised, but the air around him tightened. His eyes burned.
“You speak of justice while you hide your face and leave bodies in alleys.” His voice dropped, rough with conviction. “All Might does not kill because he is the standard. He overcomes. He inspires. He proves that evil can be stopped without becoming it. The moment a hero decides who deserves to die, he stops being a hero and becomes another tyrant with a quirk.”
He took one step closer. The scarf shifted like it wanted to move on its own.
“I purge the fakes so the true ones can stand taller. I do not question the Symbol. If your conviction is that death is the only answer, then you are no better than the villains you cut down. Prove otherwise.” The blade lifted a fraction. “Or bleed for the insult.”
The sirens were closer now. Curtis’s coat didn’t move. He faced the Hero Killer in the empty street, the low, steady pull in his chest as quiet as ever, while Stain waited for either an explanation or a fight.
Curtis’s voice stayed flat through the filter.
“You’re the same. You kill heroes. Who died and made us judges of who should live?”
The blade in Stain’s hand trembled—not from fear. From the force of the words hitting something he rarely allowed close. His eyes narrowed to slits. For a moment the usual sermon stalled.
“I do not judge for pleasure.” The answer came rough, immediate. “I judge because this society has forgotten what the title demands. Fake heroes breed more fakes. They teach the next generation that a smile and a sponsorship are enough. I stain my hands so the word ‘hero’ can mean something again. That is not the same as slaughter for its own sake.”
He stepped closer. The point of the knife now aimed at Curtis’s chest.
“You hide your face. You leave no name. You question the Symbol himself. If you have no conviction beyond ‘finish the job,’ then you are simply another killer. And killers do not get to lecture me.”
The sirens were only a few blocks away. Stain’s stance had shifted—still ready, but the ideological fire now had an edge of genuine offense. He waited, scarf shifting, for Curtis to either draw or explain why his blood-soaked nights were different from the ones Stain had spent years carving into the hero rankings.
Curtis ripped the helmet free and let it hit the pavement.
“Remember my face.”
He closed the distance in one step, seized the wrappings across Stain’s face, and tore them away. Bandages and the red mask underneath came off in a single violent pull. Before Stain could recover, Curtis slammed their foreheads together hard enough to split skin.
Blood ran between them. Neither blinked.
“You kill fake heroes,” Curtis snarled, voice raw and inches from Stain’s mouth. “I kill villains. Tell me how the hell we’re different. I don’t do this shit for pleasure.”
Stain’s eyes burned through the pain. The blade between them shook. For once the Hero Killer had no ready scripture—only the taste of his own blood and the unmasked student daring him to answer while the sirens finally turned onto their street.
The sirens turned the corner. Red and blue light washed over both of them. Stain’s eyes flicked toward the approaching cars, then back to Curtis. Blood still ran from the split on his brow. The blade stayed up, but the moment for a clean answer had already passed.
“Police,” he said, voice low. “They will not understand either of us.”
Curtis didn’t move. Foreheads still nearly touching. “Then leave.”
Stain held the stare one second longer. Something unreadable moved behind his eyes—offense, interest, the refusal to be lectured by a student who killed as cleanly as he did. Then he stepped back. The knife vanished into its sheath. He pulled the torn wrappings up just enough to cover the lower half of his face again.
“This is not finished.”
He was gone before the first patrol car fully stopped—scarf snapping once as he vaulted the nearest fence and disappeared into the alleys. Curtis stood alone in the wash of emergency lights, helmet at his feet, blood on his face, coat heavy with the night’s work.
An officer’s voice cracked over a loudspeaker. “Hands where we can see them!”
Curtis exhaled, bent, and picked up the helmet. He didn’t run. He just sealed it back over his head, red slits lighting, and turned toward the nearest shadow while Jeeves quietly marked three new escape routes in his ear. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed exactly where it always was.
Stain moved through the alleys without sound, the torn wrappings pulled tight again. The split on his brow still bled. He didn’t wipe it.
He looked me in the eye and called All Might disgusting.
The thought burned. All Might was the standard. The only one who had ever carried the title without corruption. To hear it dismissed by a masked killer who left bodies in warehouses felt like blasphemy. And yet—
He does not chase cameras. He does not leave survivors who can free the next threat. He asked who made us judges.
Stain’s hand tightened on a blade hilt. The boy had turned the question back on him without flinching. No speech. No excuse about society. Just the flat claim that finishing villains and finishing fake heroes were the same work, and that neither of them did it for pleasure.
He is not a hero. He is not a villain playing at ideology. He is something that simply removes problems and then asks why the Symbol allows them to remain.
The sirens faded behind him. Stain paused on a rooftop, city lights stretching out, and let the blood run. The conviction that had driven him for years did not crack. But for the first time in a long while, someone had met it with equal weight and refused to kneel.
I will see that face again.
He dropped into the next alley and kept moving, the question Curtis had left him still sitting behind his teeth, unanswered.
Stain stopped on the next rooftop. The wind pulled at the torn wrappings. He could still feel the impact of the headbutt, still see the heterochromia staring back at him without fear.
I could have killed him.
The admission sat cold. Speed, blades, Bloodcurdle—he had the tools. The boy had been close enough. One clean cut, one taste of blood, and the argument would have ended. He hadn’t taken it.
Not because I couldn’t. Because he is right.
The thought tasted like iron. Every villain All Might had left breathing was a future body. Every “captured” threat that later escaped was proof. Stain had spent years cutting down fake heroes so the real ones could stand taller, yet he had never turned the same blade on the cockroaches the Symbol allowed to keep living. The boy had simply asked why.
If I kill him for speaking that truth, I become the thing I hunt—someone who silences a necessary question to protect an image.
His hand loosened on the knife. The conviction did not break. All Might was still the standard. Fakes still needed purging. But the unknown hero who removed villains without an audience and then dared to call the Symbol’s mercy a flaw… that one he could not cut down. Not tonight. Not while the question still had weight.
Stain exhaled, long and slow, and dropped into the alley below. The blood on his brow had begun to dry. The face he had been told to remember stayed behind his eyes, unanswered, as he vanished into the city’s darker veins.
Curtis moved through the side streets with the helmet back on, red slits dimmed. Police lights still flashed a few blocks behind him. Jeeves stayed quiet. The blood on his forehead had already started to dry under the seal.
He actually listened.
The thought sat plain. Stain had drawn the blade. Had every reason to use it after the All Might line. He hadn’t. The headbutt, the unmasking, the flat question about judges—none of it had earned a killing stroke. That meant something.
He kills heroes he thinks are fakes. I kill villains who already proved they’ll do it again. He says it’s for the title. I say it’s so people stop dying. Same streets. Same blood. Different lists.
Curtis rolled his neck, the coat shifting. Stain’s eyes had held real offense when All Might was insulted. Real hesitation when the logic turned around. Not a performance. The man believed every word he said. That made him more dangerous than the warehouse thugs, and harder to dismiss.
He’ll come back. Next time he might decide the question isn’t enough.
The suite wasn’t far. Curtis kept walking, boots even on the pavement, the low steady pull in his chest as quiet as ever while the Hero Killer’s face stayed behind his eyes—unmasked, bleeding, and still unresolved.
Curtis kept to the side streets until the police lights faded. The helmet stayed on until he reached the building. He slipped in through the basement entrance, coat heavy, boots quiet on the concrete steps.
Inside, the suite was dark except for the single lamp he’d left on. The soundproof panels were still half-finished. The speaker sat silent on the counter. He set the helmet on the table, peeled the coat off, and dropped it over a chair. Blood had dried at his hairline. The bandage on his hand needed changing. He didn’t bother yet.
He locked the door—properly—and stood for a moment in the quiet. The low, steady pull in his chest was still there. Outside, the city kept moving. Stain was somewhere in it. So were the rest of the markers Jeeves hadn’t cleared.
Curtis turned off the lamp, stretched out on the new mattress still half in its plastic, and closed his eyes. Sleep didn’t come fast. When it did, the nightmares waited like they always did. The suite stayed locked around him while the night finished passing.
